WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.023
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.023 --> 00:00:05.309
Imagine pitching an AI helper for students who are new to algebra.

00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:12.922
The idea sounds exciting, but a school needs to know exactly what the helper will do and when a person should step in.

00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:21.813
As you watch, keep one question in mind: Trust grows when a useful idea is tested carefully and people stay responsible for the decisions.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:24.692
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:32.112
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:36.760
A good proposal is more like a small pilot than a grand promise.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:43.676
Start with one problem, gather evidence, and make a plan for what happens if the first version disappoints.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:47.262
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:47.262 --> 00:00:54.242
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:54.242 --> 00:00:57.252
Connect a task to inputs, outputs, and limits.

00:00:57.252 --> 00:01:00.838
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:01.388 --> 00:01:06.663
A student gets an answer that looks polished but uses a method the class has not learned.

00:01:06.663 --> 00:01:12.295
The school needs a way to check the explanation and a person to help when it causes confusion.

00:01:12.295 --> 00:01:13.480
Pause for a moment.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.859
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:20.593
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:20.593 --> 00:01:22.609
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:22.609 --> 00:01:27.884
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:34.079
A helper that explains one practice problem is different from one that completes graded work.

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:39.480
A clear boundary makes it easier for students and teachers to know what the tool is for.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.758
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:48.524
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:51.194
Create an evaluation and human-review plan.

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:54.836
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:54.836 --> 00:01:58.842
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:59.392 --> 00:02:05.988
Try the idea with new examples, ask students what was helpful, and record mistakes as well as successes.

00:02:05.988 --> 00:02:10.111
Decide in advance what result would make the team change course.

00:02:10.111 --> 00:02:14.487
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:14.487 --> 00:02:18.102
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:18.102 --> 00:02:24.064
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:24.614 --> 00:02:29.378
If students stop attempting problems and copy the hints, what would you change?

00:02:29.378 --> 00:02:34.203
A useful proposal includes a way to notice when the tool is getting in the way.

00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:38.003
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:38.003 --> 00:02:44.637
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:44.637 --> 00:02:48.315
Document contributions and make a reasoned release decision.

00:02:48.315 --> 00:02:52.718
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:53.268 --> 00:03:02.799
A pilot could offer optional hints for one algebra unit, keep student names out of test data, and ask learners to solve a new problem on their own afterward.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:04.741
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:04.741 --> 00:03:07.473
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:07.473 --> 00:03:12.633
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:15.547
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:15.547 --> 00:03:20.100
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.251
Now open the practice activity, Put the project workflow in order, when you are ready.

00:03:26.251 --> 00:03:36.280
As you organize the project workflow, make every step answer a real question: what problem, what evidence, whose needs, and what happens after a mistake?

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.950
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.118
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:43.118 --> 00:03:47.026
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:47.576 --> 00:03:50.969
Your lesson project is called Your one-page AI proposal.

00:03:50.969 --> 00:03:54.846
Your final proposal should make a small promise it can support.

00:03:54.846 --> 00:03:59.390
Include a limit, a way to test it, and a human contact students can reach.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.995
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:03.995 --> 00:04:05.812
What would they need to know?

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:09.084
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:09.084 --> 00:04:12.416
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:12.966 --> 00:04:18.049
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:20.781
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:20.781 --> 00:04:24.276
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:24.276 --> 00:04:28.088
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:28.088 --> 00:04:33.870
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:42.119
Here is one idea to take with you: Trust grows when a useful idea is tested carefully and people stay responsible for the decisions.

00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:46.959
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:46.959 --> 00:04:51.334
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:56.116
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:56.666 --> 00:04:59.126
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:04:59.126 --> 00:05:05.126
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:05.126 --> 00:05:11.066
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:11.066 --> 00:05:16.946
Trust grows when a useful idea is tested carefully and people stay responsible for the decisions.

00:05:16.946 --> 00:05:19.286
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:19.286 --> 00:05:23.186
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
